{:edb/db-before-t 2425 :edb/db-after-t 2426 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535738 50 #inst "2026-10-09T06:17:30.765Z" 13194139535738 true] [17592186133424 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069920/assets/f6fb8d85c6e0921d49f16b410a3c3d7c549caabb166b0e65bb4c1982844310c3.png)\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a square matrix\n\n- $A \\cdot A^T = I$\n\n- $A$ is an orthogonal matrix" 13194139535738 true] [17592186133424 1100 "$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $A$ is a square matrix\n2. $A \\cdot A^T = I$\n3. $A$ is an orthogonal matrix" 13194139535738 false] [17592186133424 1145 "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA \\cdot A^T = A^T\\cdot A = I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Statement III is true. Since $A\\cdot A^T = I$, our matrix is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535738 false] [17592186133424 1145 "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA \\cdot A^T = A^T\\cdot A = I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069920/assets/3e419059afe53ef90b1c086f75c29513e7f6cc269ac4ecbf16e0db919222b3f5.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $A\\cdot A^T = I,$ our matrix is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139535738 true] [17592186133425 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069920/assets/08c339defb7800dfbafef33db26281a6eb44740a61ff9d47bae8e9adc6af5f75.png)\n\nFind $|\\textrm{det}(PQ)|$ given that $P$ is an orthogonal matrix." 13194139535738 true] [17592186133425 1100 "$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind $\\mid \\textrm{det}(PQ) \\mid$ given that $P$ is an orthogonal matrix." 13194139535738 false] [17592186133425 1145 "We use the following facts:\n\n- The determinant of a product is equal to the product of the determinants.\n- The determinant of an orthogonal matrix $P$ is equal to $\\pm1$.\n\nApplying these ideas, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}det(PQ) & &= & det(P) \\cdot det(Q)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\mid (± 1) \\cdot det(Q) \\mid \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}± 1 & \\cdot & det(Q)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\mid det(Q) \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid (-2\\sqrt{3}) \\cdot 3\\sqrt{3} - 2 \\cdot 1 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid - 18 - 2 \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid - 20 \\mid \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535738 false] [17592186133425 1145 "We use the following facts: \n\n- The determinant of a product is equal to the product of the determinants.\n\n- The determinant of an orthogonal matrix $P$ is equal to $\\pm1.$\n\nApplying these ideas, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\text{det}(PQ)| & =|\\text{det}(P)\\cdot \\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|(\\pm 1)\\cdot \\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|\\pm 1|\\cdot |\\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|\\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|(-2\\sqrt{3})\\cdot 3\\sqrt{3}-2\\cdot 1| \\\\  & =|-18-2| \\\\  & =|-20| \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535738 true] [17592186133426 1100 "Find the inverse of the orthogonal matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069920/assets/0fdcc6a3e329666b00a45c05292d85ab2df6366ff38d5506233b6c15064d2149.png)" 13194139535738 true] [17592186133426 1100 "Find the inverse of the orthogonal matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535738 false] [17592186133426 1145 "The inverse of an orthogonal matrix is equal to its transpose.\n\nTherefore, since $D$ is orthogonal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD^{-1} &= D^{T} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535738 false] [17592186133426 1145 "The inverse of an orthogonal matrix is equal to its transpose.\n\nTherefore, since $D$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{-1} & ={D}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535738 true] [17592186133427 1100 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrices $P$ and $Q$?\n\n1. $2Q$ is orthogonal\n2. $P^{-1}Q$ is orthogonal\n3. $PQ^T$ is orthogonal" 13194139535738 false] [17592186133427 1100 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrices $P$ and $Q?$\n\n- $2Q$ is orthogonal\n\n- $P^{-1}Q$ is orthogonal\n\n- $PQ^T$ is orthogonal" 13194139535738 true] [17592186133427 1145 "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ Q^T = Q^T Q = I\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q$, we have $Q^{-1} = Q^T$.\n- $(AB)^T = B^TA^T$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the product $2Q \\cdot (2Q)^T$, we obtain\n$2Q \\cdot (2Q)^{T}|= 2Q \\cdot 2Q^{T}; = 4QQ^{T}; = 4I; \\ne I$.\n- Statement II is true. Computing the product $(P^{-1}Q)\\cdot (P^{-1}Q)^T$, we obtain\n$(P^{-1}Q) \\cdot (P^{-1}Q)^{T}|= (P^{T}Q) \\cdot (P^{T}Q)^{T}; = P^{T}Q \\cdot Q^{T}(P^{T})^{T}; = P^{T}(QQ^{T})P; = P^{T}(I)P; = P^{T}P; = I$.\n- Statement III is true. Computing the product $(PQ^T)\\cdot(PQ^T)^T$, we obtain\n$(PQ^{T}) \\cdot (PQ^{T})^{T}|= PQ^{T} \\cdot (Q^{T})^{T}P^{T}; = PQ^{T}QP^{T}; = P(Q^{T}Q)P^{T}; = P(I)P^{T}; = PP^{T}; = I$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535738 false] [17592186133427 1145 "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ Q^T = Q^T Q = I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have $Q^{-1} = Q^T.$\n\n- $(AB)^T = B^TA^T$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the product $2Q \\cdot (2Q)^T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2Q\\cdot (2Q)^{T} & =2Q\\cdot 2{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =4Q{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =4I \\\\  & \\ne I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the product $(P^{-1}Q)\\cdot (P^{-1}Q)^T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({P}^{-1}Q)\\cdot ({P}^{-1}Q)^{T} & =({P}^{T}Q)\\cdot ({P}^{T}Q)^{T} \\\\  & ={P}^{T}Q\\cdot {Q}^{T}({P}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={P}^{T}(Q{Q}^{T})P \\\\  & ={P}^{T}(I)P \\\\  & ={P}^{T}P \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Computing the product $(PQ^T)\\cdot(PQ^T)^T ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(P{Q}^{T})\\cdot (P{Q}^{T})^{T} & =P{Q}^{T}\\cdot ({Q}^{T})^{T}{P}^{T} \\\\  & =P{Q}^{T}Q{P}^{T} \\\\  & =P({Q}^{T}Q){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P(I){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P{P}^{T} \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535738 true] [17592186133867 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364053 13194139535738 true] [17592186133867 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364060 13194139535738 true] [17592186133867 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364067 13194139535738 true] [17592186133867 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364074 13194139535738 true] [17592186133867 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364081 13194139535738 true] [17592186133868 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364088 13194139535738 true] [17592186133868 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364095 13194139535738 true] [17592186133868 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364130 13194139535738 true] [17592186133868 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364137 13194139535738 true] [17592186133868 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364144 13194139535738 true] [17592186133869 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364039 13194139535738 true] [17592186133869 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364102 13194139535738 true] [17592186133869 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364109 13194139535738 true] [17592186133869 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364151 13194139535738 true] [17592186133869 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364158 13194139535738 true] [17592186133870 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364046 13194139535738 true] [17592186133870 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364116 13194139535738 true] [17592186133870 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364123 13194139535738 true] [17592186133870 1020 #edb/ref 17592186364165 13194139535738 true] [17592186134396 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535738 true] [17592186134396 1100 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the orthogonal matrix $Q$?\n\n1. $Q^{T}$ is orthogonal\n2. The identity matrix $I$ is orthogonal\n3. $2Q$ is orthogonal" 13194139535738 false] [17592186134396 1100 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the orthogonal matrix $Q?$\n\n- ${Q}^{T}$ is orthogonal\n- The identity matrix $I$ is orthogonal\n- $2Q$ is orthogonal" 13194139535738 true] [17592186134396 1145 "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the product ${Q}^{T}\\cdot ({Q}^{T})^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{T}\\cdot ({Q}^{T})^{T} & ={Q}^{T}Q \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The columns of the identity matrix are orthonormal. Therefore, $I$ is an orthogonal matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. Computing the product $2Q\\cdot (2Q)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2Q\\cdot (2Q)^{T} & =2Q\\cdot 2{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =4Q{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =4I \\\\  & \\ne I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364039 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364040 13194139535738 true] [17592186364039 1089 "q-140563" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364039 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535738 true] [17592186364039 1100 "Solve for $x$ in $Ax=b$ given that the matrix $A$ is orthogonal, and \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15} & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} & \\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{15}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364039 1113 #uuid "e8533074-b38e-59e9-9dd6-3ff9a3c53ce5" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364039 1145 "The inverse of an orthogonal matrix is equal to its transpose.\n\nHence, since $A$ is orthogonal, we have ${A}^{-1}={A}^{T}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ {A}^{-1}Ax & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ x & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{15}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364040 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364041 13194139535738 true] [17592186364040 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364042 13194139535738 true] [17592186364040 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364043 13194139535738 true] [17592186364040 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364044 13194139535738 true] [17592186364040 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364045 13194139535738 true] [17592186364040 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535738 true] [17592186364040 1041 "selection" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364040 1104 #edb/ref 17592186364044 13194139535738 true] [17592186364040 1110 #uuid "7cdbc981-0b9d-5fe2-a5f2-ded978f7ce8e" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364041 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364041 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535738 true] [17592186364041 1111 #uuid "961c7b4f-ef86-51c6-a567-18444982003c" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364042 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364042 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535738 true] [17592186364042 1111 #uuid "92ab6b74-455d-52ed-81ca-fa05c01da14a" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364043 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364043 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535738 true] [17592186364043 1111 #uuid "0e9fbaa6-d3fa-5b03-a34e-3ba117b9469e" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364044 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364044 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535738 true] [17592186364044 1111 #uuid "850f2dd8-04ca-5ba5-b0f4-64fe737c2868" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364045 1034 "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364045 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535738 true] [17592186364045 1111 #uuid "9e8b40f6-14d3-5353-a75a-61819fb4a0af" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364046 1017 #edb/ref 17592186364047 13194139535738 true] [17592186364046 1089 "q-140565" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364046 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535738 true] [17592186364046 1100 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrix $Q$ and the $n\\times n$ matrix $P?$\n\n- If $\\lambda $ is a real eigenvalue of $Q,$ then $\\lambda =\\pm 1$\n- If the determinant of $P$ is either $1$ or $-1,$ then $P$ is orthogonal\n- $Q-I$ is singular" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364046 1113 #uuid "b2aa8f69-b3a1-5fb8-98eb-33d753ac84cc" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364046 1145 "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. If $\\lambda $ is an eigenvalue of an orthogonal matrix $Q$ then $\\lambda =\\pm 1$\n\n- Statement II is false. Every orthogonal matrix has the determinant equal to $\\pm 1$, but not every matrix with the determinant equal to $\\pm 1$ is orthogonal. As a counterexample, consider the following matrix:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe determinant of $P$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P)=2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 1=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n however, $P$ is not orthogonal since, for example, the first column is not normalized.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, consider the following orthogonal matrix:\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nQ-I=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{5} & -\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand $Q-I$ is not singular since, for example, it doesn't have proportional rows or columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364047 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364048 13194139535738 true] [17592186364047 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364049 13194139535738 true] [17592186364047 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364050 13194139535738 true] [17592186364047 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364051 13194139535738 true] [17592186364047 1000 #edb/ref 17592186364052 13194139535738 true] [17592186364047 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535738 true] [17592186364047 1041 "selection" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364047 1104 #edb/ref 17592186364050 13194139535738 true] [17592186364047 1110 #uuid "ba2d3ced-f79f-5cec-aacd-52a028f2642c" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364048 1034 "None of the statements are true" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364048 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535738 true] [17592186364048 1111 #uuid "944c9bb1-202d-516e-a9d2-f5d0b420d7f9" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364049 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364049 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535738 true] [17592186364049 1111 #uuid "93ced2ef-d9b0-5f5c-b92f-0822a010768d" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364050 1034 "I only" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364050 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535738 true] [17592186364050 1111 #uuid "148bf90b-85db-5260-abad-23f78e5d3078" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364051 1034 "III only" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364051 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535738 true] [17592186364051 1111 #uuid "6e855fa1-6795-527a-9414-7dbacffd420c" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364052 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535738 true] [17592186364052 1103